<h1>Physics layers</h1>
<p>Layers decide <strong>which bodies collide with which</strong>. Without them, everything hits everything. With them, players pass through pickups, bullets ignore their own team, and triggers only notice the objects you care about.</p>

<h2>Declare the collision matrix</h2>
<p><code>physics.layers</code> is an <strong>object</strong>: each key is a layer name, and its value is the list of layer names it collides with.</p>
<pre><code>"physics": {
  "layers": {
    "goal": ["player"],
    "player": ["goal", "wall"],
    "wall": ["player"]
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>Read it like a sentence: the <code>goal</code> layer collides with <code>player</code>; <code>player</code> collides with <code>goal</code> and <code>wall</code>; <code>wall</code> collides with <code>player</code>. Rules:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Each key is a layer name. Every collider picks exactly one of these names via its <code>layer</code> field.</li>
  <li>The value lists the layers that key collides with. <strong>Both sides must accept each other</strong>: a contact only happens when each layer lists the other. In the example above, <code>player</code> lists <code>goal</code> and <code>goal</code> lists <code>player</code>, so they meet. If either side omits the other, the pair is ignored — no collision, no trigger, no contact event.</li>
  <li>A layer name only needs to appear as a key once. Listing a name in a value without declaring it as a key is not enough to create the layer.</li>
  <li>Maximum 16 properties.</li>
  <li>Authored order does <strong>not</strong> assign bit indices. The parser sorts layers by name, so the same matrix always resolves the same way regardless of how the keys are written.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you omit <code>physics.layers</code>, everything sits on a single implicit <code>default</code> layer and all bodies collide with each other.</p>

<h2>How filtering works</h2>
<p>Every collider carries a <code>layer</code> name. When two bodies overlap, the engine looks up both names in the matrix and asks one question: does each side list the other?</p>
<pre><code>layers[colliderA.layer]  contains colliderB.layer
layers[colliderB.layer]  contains colliderA.layer</code></pre>
<p>If both answers are yes, the contact is generated. If either says no, the pair is ignored. That is the whole authoring story — no bit math, no masks to maintain by hand.</p>
<p>Colliders also expose low-level <code>category_bits</code> / <code>mask_bits</code> fields for special cases, but they are derived from the matrix and are not how you author collision rules. Prefer the matrix.</p>

<h2>Worked example</h2>
<pre><code>"physics": {
  "layers": {
    "player": ["world", "enemy", "pickup"],
    "enemy": ["world", "player"],
    "world": ["player", "enemy"],
    "pickup": ["player"]
  }
}</code></pre>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Body</th><th><code>layer</code></th><th>Collides with</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Player</td><td><code>player</code></td><td>World, enemies, pickups</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Enemy</td><td><code>enemy</code></td><td>World and player, not other enemies</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Bullet</td><td><code>enemy</code></td><td>Same as enemy — enemy bullets hit the player</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Wall</td><td><code>world</code></td><td>Player and enemies</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Coin</td><td><code>pickup</code></td><td>Player only</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Enemies walk through each other because <code>enemy</code> does not list <code>enemy</code>. Coins never bounce off walls because <code>pickup</code> lists only <code>player</code>, and <code>world</code> does not list <code>pickup</code>.</p>

<h2>Triggers and layers</h2>
<pre><code>{
  "CircleCollider2D": {
    "radius": 1.5,
    "is_trigger": true,
    "layer": "pickup"
  }
}</code></pre>
<p>A trigger uses the same matrix. It generates <code>physics_trigger_enter/stay/exit</code> instead of solid collision. A pickup area that should only notice the player sits on a layer that lists only <code>player</code> — and <code>player</code> must list that layer back.</p>

<h2>2D and 3D</h2>
<p>This matrix is the 2D collision matrix (it drives the Box2D world). 3D colliders also take a <code>layer</code> string field, but there is no separate 3D matrix in the project file. Keep the layer names coherent so 2D HUD hit areas and 3D world bodies can share vocabulary.</p>

<h2>Debugging filters</h2>
<pre><code>demi run \
  --debug-overlays colliders,contacts</code></pre>
<p>Colliders draw in their layer. If two shapes overlap and no contact appears, check the matrix on both sides — a one-way listing is the usual cause.</p>

<h2>Choosing layers</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Start with <code>player</code>, <code>enemy</code>, <code>world</code>, <code>pickup</code>.</li>
  <li>Add <code>trigger</code>, <code>debris</code>, <code>ragdoll</code> as needed.</li>
  <li>Prefer a few broad layers over one layer per entity type.</li>
  <li>Remember the 16-layer ceiling when designing large games.</li>
  <li>Keep the matrix symmetric where you want contact: if A should hit B, list each in the other's value.</li>
</ul>
